 "standard_text": "The distributive property, multiplying expressions, and the identities (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b² and (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b² (summary of NCERT Ganita Prakash Class 8)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 8 We Distribute, Yet Things Multiply worksheet (Ganita Prakash Part 1, Chapter 6): distributive property, expanding brackets and algebraic identities. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "a(b + c) = ab + ac",
 "key_sub": "(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²; (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b².",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>The <b>distributive property</b>: a(b + c) = ab + ac. Multiply every term in the bracket.</p><p>For two brackets, multiply each term by each term: (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd.</p><p><b>Identities</b>: (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b², (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b². They make mental sums easy: 103 × 97 = 100² − 3² = 9,991.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Expand 3x(2x − 5).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3x × 2x − 3x × 5</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 6x² − 15x</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find 98² using an identity.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(100 − 2)² = 10,000 − 400 + 4</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 9,604</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Writing (a + b)² = a² + b² and forgetting 2ab.</li><li>Multiplying only the first term: 3(x + 4) = 3x + 4.</li><li>Losing the sign: −2(x − 5) = −2x + 10, not −2x − 10.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the distributive property?",
   "a": "a(b + c) = ab + ac: multiplying a sum is the same as multiplying each part and adding."
  },
  {
   "q": "What are the standard identities?",
   "a": "(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b², (a − b)² = a² − 2ab + b² and (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b²."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do identities help with mental maths?",
   "a": "For example 51² = (50 + 1)² = 2,500 + 100 + 1 = 2,601, and 47 × 53 = 50² − 3² = 2,491."
  }
